---
// Three cases in depth, for a reader deciding whether the person who built this can do
// the work. Ten shallow entries read as nothing finished; three deep ones read as
// focused. The other seven are a strip at the bottom, which is the right weight for them.
//
// Every figure comes from openstudy/src/data/work.json, which ops/work extracts from the
// records the pipelines wrote. Nothing here is typed by hand, and `ops/work check` fails
// if the extract drifts from the pipelines.
//
// The three are not a sequence, so they are not numbered. They are labelled by what each
// one demonstrates, because that is the only ordering a reader of this page cares about.
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import W from "../data/work.json";
import S from "../data/stack.json";
import { INSTRUMENTS } from "../data/instruments.js";

const P = W.poverty, R = W.rice, H = W.haze;
const n = (x) => x.toLocaleString("en-US");
const pc = (x) => `${(x * 100).toFixed(0)}%`;
const f2 = (x) => x.toFixed(2);
const f3 = (x) => x.toFixed(3);
const blob = (f) => `${S.blob}/${f}`;

const DEEP = ["poverty", "rice", "haze"];
const rest = INSTRUMENTS.filter((i) => !DEEP.includes(i.slug));
---

<Page
  title="Work"
  description="Three cases in depth — spatial validation on a poverty model, a controlled experiment on a radar harvest detector, and day-ahead haze risk scored against an operational index — plus seven more."
  current="/work/"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Three in depth · {rest.length} more built</p>
    <h1>Public data, Indonesia,<br /><em>and what the analysis caught.</em></h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Each of these three is a live dashboard, a pipeline that runs from raw satellite
      products, and a result that contradicted what the work was expected to show. They are
      here rather than the other seven because each one demonstrates something different,
      and because the figure that matters in all three came out of the evaluation rather
      than the model.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────────  POVERTY  ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec case" id="poverty">
    <div class="rail">Validation design<span>{P.units} regencies</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">
      <p class="topic">Poverty mapping from space</p>
      <h2>The same model scores {f2(P.validation[0].r2)}, {f2(P.validation[1].r2)} or
        {f2(P.validation[2].r2)} — depending only on how the data is split</h2>
      <p>
        A LightGBM model predicting regency poverty rate from {P.features} features across
        five families — {Object.entries(P.families).map(([k, v]) => `${k} (${v})`).join(", ")} —
        built from night-time lights, building footprints, land cover and population.
        Nothing about the model changes between these three rows. Only the fold design
        does.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Validation design</th><th class="num">Folds</th><th class="num">R²</th><th>What it answers</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {P.validation.map((v, i) => (
              <tr class={i === P.validation.length - 1 ? "keep" : ""}>
                <td><b>{v.design}</b></td>
                <td class="num dim">{v.folds}</td>
                <td class="num big">{f3(v.r2)}</td>
                <td class="dim">{v.what}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Choosing the first row would have been defensible, conventional, and wrong by
        <b>0.26 of R²</b>. Neighbouring regencies are similar, so a random fold leaves the
        answer next door; blocking at 200 km removes some of that, and holding out a whole
        province removes it properly. <b>{f3(P.validation[2].r2)} is the number a reader
        gets.</b>
      </p>

      <h3>Where the remaining skill actually comes from</h3>
      <p>
        Reporting the honest R² is not the same as understanding it, so the case
        decomposes it — and the answer is uncomfortable.
      </p>
      <ul class="findings">
        <li><b>{pc(P.offset_share_of_sse)} of the squared error is a single constant per
          province.</b> The model is substantially recovering each province's own offset
          rather than seeing poverty.</li>
        <li><b>Within a province, R² is {f3(P.within_province_r2)}.</b> Rank a province's
          own regencies against each other and the model is close to blind — which is
          exactly the comparison a targeting decision would need.</li>
        <li><b>On cities, R² is {f3(P.kota_r2)}</b> against {f3(P.kabupaten_r2)} on rural
          regencies. Worse than predicting the mean. A single national figure hides that
          completely.</li>
        <li><b>A naive temporal split reports {f3(P.temporal.naive)}.</b> Removing the
          leakage takes the same model to {f3(P.temporal.strict)}. Both numbers are
          published, because the first one is the one most write-ups would print.</li>
        <li><b>Ridge on the same features and the same folds: {f3(P.ridge_lopo_r2)}.</b>
          The gradient boosting is earning its complexity — which is worth checking rather
          than assuming.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="demo">
        <b>What this demonstrates.</b> Knowing that a validation design is a modelling
        decision, that a spatial target leaks through geography, and that a headline metric
        can be almost entirely composed of structure the model was handed rather than
        signal it found.
      </p>
      <p class="go">
        <a class="strong" href="/poverty/">Open the dashboard<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        <a href="/poverty/article/">Method and validation<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        <a href={blob("cases/poverty-map/pipeline/model.py")}>model.py<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        <a href={blob("cases/poverty-map/pipeline/review.py")}>review.py<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────────  RICE  ─────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec case" id="rice">
    <div class="rail">Data engineering<span>{n(R.scenes)} radar scenes</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">
      <p class="topic">Java's rice harvest, from radar</p>
      <h2>Precision {f2(R.precision)}, recall {f2(R.recall)} — and a controlled experiment
        showing why</h2>
      <p>
        {n(R.scenes)} Sentinel-1 scenes over {R.regencies} of Java's largest rice
        regencies, {R.first} to {R.last}, reduced to per-cell backscatter time series —
        {n(R.cells_one_regency)} cells × {R.acquisitions_one_regency} acquisitions in one
        regency alone, a median of {R.median_obs_per_cell} observations per cell. C-band
        radar because it sees through monsoon cloud, which optical cannot.
      </p>
      <p>
        The detector agrees with an independent rice map {pc(R.precision)} of the time on
        the cells it flags, and finds {pc(R.recall)} of the cells that map calls rice.
        <b>Precision is the one statistic a detector can always buy by finding fewer
        fields</b>, so the interesting question is what limits recall. The published
        explanation was cell size. It is not.
      </p>

      <h3>The experiment: same fields, same detector, fewer looks</h3>
      <p>
        {R.thinning.kabupaten}, {n(R.thinning.cells)} known rice cells,
        {" "}{R.thinning.seasons} seasons. Acquisitions are removed from the record and the
        unchanged detector re-runs. Soil, variety, weather, thresholds and code are all
        held constant — only the revisit interval moves.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th class="num">Median gap</th><th class="num">Acquisitions</th><th class="num">Crop cycles found</th><th class="num">vs full record</th><th class="num">Extent recall</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {R.thinning.ladder.map((l, i) => (
              <tr class={i === 0 ? "keep" : ""}>
                <td class="num"><b>{l.gap_days} days</b></td>
                <td class="num dim">{l.acquisitions}</td>
                <td class="num">{n(l.cycles)}</td>
                <td class="num big">{pc(l.cycles_vs_full)}</td>
                <td class="num dim">{pc(l.extent_recall)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Doubling the revisit interval from {R.thinning.ladder[0].gap_days} to
        {" "}{R.thinning.ladder[1].gap_days} days costs
        {" "}<b>{pc(1 - R.thinning.ladder[1].cycles_vs_full)} of the crop cycles</b>. The
        binding constraint is how often the satellite looks, not how finely it resolves —
        and across regencies the correlation between revisit gap and extent recall is
        {" "}<b>{f2(R.revisit_corr)}</b>.
      </p>

      <h3>What it is actually good for</h3>
      <ul class="findings">
        <li><b>The date, not the hectares.</b> Monthly area correlates with the official
          series at {f2(R.timing.xcorr_at_zero)} — near nothing. Allow a
          {" "}{R.timing.lag_months}-month systematic lag and the same series correlates at
          {" "}<b>{f2(R.timing.xcorr_at_lag)}</b>. The instrument recovers <em>when</em> the
          harvest lands, which is the more useful product, and not the quantity it was
          built to estimate.</li>
        <li><b>Calibration, scored on a hold-out rather than in-sample.</b> Uncalibrated
          monthly R² {f2(R.calibration.uncalibrated_r2)} → in-sample
          {" "}{f2(R.calibration.in_sample_r2)} → <b>hold-out
          {" "}{f2(R.calibration.holdout_r2)}</b>. Reporting only the middle number is the
          usual way this gets published.</li>
        <li><b>The benchmark was checked before it was trusted.</b> The official survey
          changed method in {R.benchmark_break_year}, a {R.benchmark_break_pct}% step —
          {" "}four years before this record opens on {R.record_opens}, so it cannot explain
          the shortfall. Ruling that out mattered more than any model change.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="demo">
        <b>What this demonstrates.</b> Satellite ingest and reduction at volume; designing
        a controlled experiment on a pipeline you already built; and being willing to
        conclude that the product is a different quantity than the one you set out to
        measure.
      </p>
      <p class="go">
        <a class="strong" href="/rice/">Open the dashboard<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        <a href="/rice/article/">Method and validation<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        <a href={blob("cases/rice-security/pipeline/ingest_sar.py")}>ingest_sar.py<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        <a href={blob("cases/rice-security/pipeline/audit.py")}>audit.py<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ──────────────────────────────  HAZE  ─────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec case" id="haze">
    <div class="rail">Operational modelling<span>{n(H.fwi_rows)} rows</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">
      <p class="topic">Fire and haze early warning</p>
      <h2>AUC {f3(H.leads[0].auc)} a day ahead, against the operational index's
        {" "}{f3(H.vs_fwi.fwi)}</h2>
      <p>
        Day-ahead fire risk over {n(H.cells)} cells, trained on {n(H.fwi_rows)} rows of
        weather and fire history across {H.fwi_years} years. The positive class is
        {" "}<b>{(H.base_rate * 100).toFixed(1)}%</b> of rows, so the model is scored on
        ranking rather than accuracy, and negatives are subsampled at
        {" "}{H.neg_sample_rate} with {H.rounds} boosting rounds.
      </p>
      <div class="tw">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Horizon</th><th class="num">Rows scored</th><th class="num">AUC</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {H.leads.map((l) => (
              <tr>
                <td><b>{l.days} day{l.days > 1 ? "s" : ""} ahead</b></td>
                <td class="num dim">{n(l.n)}</td>
                <td class="num big">{f3(l.auc)}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <ul class="findings">
        <li><b>The rival is an operational index, not nothing.</b> On the same
          {" "}{n(H.vs_fwi.n)} rows, the Fire Weather Index scores
          {" "}{f3(H.vs_fwi.fwi)} and this model {f3(H.vs_fwi.model)}. Beating a real
          incumbent is the only comparison that means anything; beating a constant is not
          a result.</li>
        <li><b>Held out on the years that matter.</b> {H.anchors.map((a) => a.year).join(" and ")}
          {" "}— the two catastrophic haze seasons — are excluded from training entirely
          and scored as anchors: AUC {H.anchors.map((a) => f3(a.auc)).join(" and ")}. A
          model that only works in ordinary years is useless for the purpose.</li>
        <li><b>Blocked in space and in season.</b> {H.spatial.folds} spatial folds over
          {" "}{H.spatial.blocks} blocks of {H.spatial.block_deg}°, plus season-held-out
          folds, because fire is autocorrelated in both dimensions and a random split
          would flatter it in both.</li>
        <li><b>Attribution that admits when it cannot attribute.</b> A back-trajectory
          ensemble over {n(H.trajectory.parcels)} parcels and
          {" "}{n(H.trajectory.receptor_days)} receptor-days names a likely source province
          for smoke — and reports that <b>{pc(H.trajectory.unattributable)} of receptor-days
          have no attributable source at all.</b> The naming is stable for
          {" "}{pc(H.trajectory.stable_at_48h)} of episodes when the trajectory is truncated
          at 48 hours, and agrees with observed bearing within 30° for
          {" "}{pc(H.trajectory.bearing_within_30)} of them.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="demo">
        <b>What this demonstrates.</b> Imbalanced ranking problems scored the way they
        should be, a baseline that is a real operational system, hold-out chosen for
        consequence rather than convenience, and a downstream attribution step that
        publishes its own failure rate.
      </p>
      <p class="go">
        <a class="strong" href="/haze/">Open the dashboard<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        <a href="/haze/article/">Method and validation<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        <a href={blob("cases/fire-haze/pipeline/risk.py")}>risk.py<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        <a href={blob("cases/fire-haze/pipeline/transport.py")}>transport.py<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ────────────────────────────  THE REST  ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="sec" id="more">
    <div class="rail">Also built<span>{rest.length} instruments</span></div>
    <div class="col wide">
      <h2>Same pipeline discipline, less depth on this page</h2>
      <p>
        Each has a live dashboard, a method article and a published set of checks. They get
        a line here rather than a section because three worked examples say more than ten
        summaries.
      </p>
      <ul class="strip">
        {rest.map((i) => (
          <li>
            <a href={`/${i.slug}/`}>{i.title}</a>
            <span>{i.line}</span>
            <em>{i.found}</em>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <b>And one replication.</b> A published thermoeconomic optimisation rebuilt from
          the paper alone — three of five pre-registered checks reproduced, two did not,
          and a compressor efficiency its own author no longer had was recovered from the
          paper's own reported performance. It is here as proof of method rather than as a
          subject: <a href="/notes/cascade-refrigeration/">the note</a>, and
          {" "}<a href="/notes/cascade-explorer/">the dashboard</a> that recomputes 62,370
          operating points in the browser.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="go">
        <a href="/stack/">What all of it is built with<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
        <a href="/standards/">How a result gets to count<span aria-hidden="true"> →</span></a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .hero { padding-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.72rem; font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent);
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
  }
  .hero h1 { max-width: 26ch; }
  .sec .col.wide { max-width: none; }
  .case .col > p { max-width: 74ch; }

  .topic { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.7rem; font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
    margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
  .case h2 { max-width: 30ch; }
  .case h3 { margin-top: 2rem; }

  td.num, th.num { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  td.dim { color: var(--muted); }
  td.big { font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); font-size: 1.02rem; }
  tr.keep td.big { color: var(--accent); }

  .findings { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0.8rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.62; color: var(--muted); }
  .findings li { padding: 0.9rem 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); max-width: 74ch; }
  .findings b { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }

  .demo { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.88rem; line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); padding: 0.2rem 0 0.2rem 1rem;
    margin: 1.8rem 0 0; max-width: 72ch; }
  .demo b { color: var(--ink); }

  .go { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.6rem 1.7rem; margin: 1.5rem 0 0;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.88rem; }
  .go a { color: var(--muted); text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); padding-bottom: 2px; }
  .go a:hover { color: var(--accent); border-bottom-color: var(--accent); }
  .go a.strong { color: var(--accent); border-bottom-color: var(--accent); font-weight: 600; }

  .strip { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 1.4rem 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .strip li { padding: 0.95rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .strip a { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 0.98rem; font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--ink); text-decoration: none; }
  .strip a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
  .strip span { display: block; margin-top: 0.2rem; font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--muted); max-width: 74ch; }
  .strip em { display: block; margin-top: 0.35rem; font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 0.82rem; font-style: normal; color: var(--muted); opacity: 0.85;
    max-width: 74ch; }
</style>
